# ─── Secrets — NEVER commit any of these ─────────────────────────────────────
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
*.crt
*.cer
*.der
*secret*
*credential*
*_secrets.json
*_credentials.json

# The two glob patterns above are for credential/secret *data*. Until 2026-07-29 they also
# matched *source*, and silently excluded from the repository: 11 Python modules — including
# `credential_client.py` (the shared base class), `connection_credential_resolver.py`, and the
# `credential_expiry_notifier_handler` Lambda — plus the whole `infrastructure/modules/secrets`
# Terraform module that all three environments reference. A fresh clone could not import and
# `terraform validate` could not run, so CI had failed on every run for months while every check
# passed on developer machines, where the files exist untracked on disk.
#
# Order matters: a file cannot be re-included while its parent directory is excluded, so the
# directory negations must come first. Real credential *data* (.json/.env/.pem) still matches the
# patterns above and stays ignored.
!*credential*/
!*secret*/
!*credential*.py
!*secret*.py
!*credential*.tf
!*secret*.tf
# `.secrets.baseline` was classed as data by the 2026-07-29 pass and left ignored. It is not data:
# it holds detect-secrets' plugin set and an empty findings map, and the CI secret-scan job passes
# it to `--baseline`. Untracked, that job failed on every run with `Invalid path: .secrets.baseline`
# — the same local-passes/CI-fails shape as the source files above, one file later.
!.secrets.baseline
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

